{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["<b>And he said, etc. — Why, Moses, etc.</b> The meaning is that Moses and Aaron and the elders stood before him, those who had said ״אֱלֹהֵי הָעִבְרִים״ etc., and he turned his face to Moses and said: my complaint is not against the elders of Israel, for they are seized by dread and fear of the sword and the pestilence; rather, the complaint is against you, for you did two things: the first — that you came to me on a mission from your Elohim. If so, the matter depends on me; if so, what place is there to frighten the people lest pestilence strike them, when they are not in their own control and are under compulsion? What will you say — that they can go of their own free will? If so, why did you come to me on a mission to send them? And perhaps it is to this that the Sages, of blessed memory, intended (Exodus Rabbah 5:15) when they said, and this is their language: ״You — why? Your words — why?״ Meaning: you — why did you come to me? Your words — that you said to this people on behalf of their Elohim, lest etc. — why? For they are two contradictory things. And he turned his face back to Israel and said <b>go to your burdens</b>, for Moses and Aaron were in their own control."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 5, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:24:46.559246775Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}